    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4a2ce114_8e4c6371",
        "filename": "board/cr50/tpm2/virtual_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1256783
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-03T08:46:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can do this for sure, but as below, this would imply the TPM implementation has changed, and seems like clipping here could lead to weird failures later that are hard to trace back to this?\n\nSorry I\u0027m not up to speed with best practices for handling this kind of error in firmware - in my previous work failing early was generally favored, to simplify debugging (but ofc realise that failing early here isn\u0027t going to leave much in the way of info to debug with)",
      "parentUuid": "78f5f797_bc43d7c7",
      "revId": "d41ec856b02f5b0e89d3e8822989f6f47cea9a9b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fad657b5_32b7c26c",
        "filename": "board/cr50/tpm2/virtual_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000229
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-03T15:49:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Frankly, I don\u0027t think we should care at this point, neither about this nor about making sure that the entire NV_INDEX space is read below.\n\nDoes TPM2 library enforce when accessing \u0027regular\u0027 indices, this just complicates the code without much benefit. \n\nAndrey, do you have an opinion?",
      "parentUuid": "4a2ce114_8e4c6371",
      "revId": "d41ec856b02f5b0e89d3e8822989f6f47cea9a9b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e36a7209_2c1c46f3",
        "filename": "board/cr50/tpm2/virtual_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-03T16:38:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My overall approach: let debug builds fail early, let prod build as robust as possible. \nWe had this discussion. I\u0027m generally suspicious of asserts (cr50 should never die alone), but here they\u0027d reboot the entire system, which is fine; so I was ok to go ahead with them.  Still, I slightly prefer if() -\u003e return. If we go with \u0027if\u0027, the only issue is that we don\u0027t have any mechanism for reporting errors like that (maybe just print on the console to help with debugging, when somebody is looking).\nBut generally up to you.",
      "parentUuid": "fad657b5_32b7c26c",
      "revId": "d41ec856b02f5b0e89d3e8822989f6f47cea9a9b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5cd85a1e_c3242b34",
        "filename": "board/cr50/tpm2/virtual_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1256783
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-07T15:31:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve re-worked this so arbitrary offsets / lengths can be read, PTAL.\n\nI\u0027m wondering if in the final case (where more data is requested than is available) we should zero the rest of the destination buffer, to avoid any chance of leaking something to users. WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "e36a7209_2c1c46f3",
      "revId": "d41ec856b02f5b0e89d3e8822989f6f47cea9a9b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1a6dd9f6_9cb14c70",
        "filename": "board/cr50/tpm2/virtual_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 269,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-07T15:39:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "good idea. since we don\u0027t know where the allocated buffer is coming from, zeroing it out sounds like a nice idea.",
      "parentUuid": "5cd85a1e_c3242b34",
      "revId": "d41ec856b02f5b0e89d3e8822989f6f47cea9a9b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
